  92:  * response shape and from nothing else.
  93:  *
  94:  * IT TAKES `unknown` ON PURPOSE, exactly as `extractGithubHandle` does. The argument is a JSON
  95:  * body that crossed a network; typing the parameter as a tidy record would be a claim about
  96:  * somebody else's server. So every step down into it is checked here instead.
  97:  *
  98:  * THE ONLY VERIFIED ANSWER is a `email_confirmed_at` that is a string with something in it. A
  99:  * missing field, `null`, an empty or blank string, a number, a boolean, an object, an array, and
 100:  * a user that is not an object at all ALL read as unverified. That list is not defensive
 101:  * decoration — it is the fail-closed direction, and it is the safe one here: the gate below
 102:  * refuses on unverified, so a shape this does not recognise BLOCKS a write loudly instead of
 103:  * waiving the floor quietly.
 104:  *
 105:  * BLANK IS UNVERIFIED, and that is a deliberate reading rather than an accident of using `trim`.
 106:  * A timestamp of three spaces is not an instant, and treating it as one would let the single
 107:  * value most likely to arrive from a broken serialiser mean "verified".
 108:  *
 109:  * WHAT IS NOT PROVED BY ANY LOOP-TIER GREEN over this function: that the shape it reads is the
 110:  * shape GoTrue really sends. The fixture renders the canonical shape, which is a prediction of
 111:  * the vendor. The binding measurement is the live proof — `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`
 112:  * check (d) feeds a REAL `/auth/v1/user` response of a confirmed user to this function.
 113:  */
 114: export function emailVerifiedFromUser(user: unknown): boolean {
 115:   if (typeof user !== 'object' || user === null) return false;
 116:   const confirmedAt = (user as Record<string, unknown>)[EMAIL_CONFIRMED_AT_FIELD];
 117:   return typeof confirmedAt === 'string' && confirmedAt.trim() !== '';
 118: }
 119: 
 120: /* ------------------------------------------------- 2. may this caller send a Discovery message */
 121: 
 122: /**
 123:  * WHAT IS TRUE OF THE CALLER, as opposed to what the caller SAID — the same distinction
 124:  * `CompleteSignupCaller` in `./accounts.ts` draws, and for the same security reason.
 125:  *
 126:  * `emailVerified` comes from `emailVerifiedFromUser` over the `/auth/v1/user` response for the
 127:  * authenticated user. It is NEVER a field of the request body: a request field is something a
 128:  * client asserts about itself, and a gate built on one gates nothing.
 129:  */
 130: export type DiscoveryMessageCaller = {
 131:   /** whether Supabase Auth reports this user's email address confirmed */
 132:   emailVerified: boolean;
 133: };
 134: 
 135: /**
 136:  * AT-001.10, as a decision: a Discovery message is permitted to a caller whose email address is
 137:  * verified, and to nobody else.
 138:  *
 139:  * THE ONLY ALLOW IS `caller?.emailVerified === true`. Strict equality against `true`, not a
 140:  * truthiness test: a caller that passed the string `"false"`, or `1`, or any other value a
 141:  * careless JSON round trip produces, must not open the gate. This is the same fail-closed posture
 142:  * `validateCompleteSignup` takes on its caller fact, for the same measured reason — the edge
 143:  * entry point has no type-checker, so a missing or malformed argument is a real possibility.
 144:  *
 145:  * THE REFUSAL NAMES EMAIL VERIFICATION TWICE: as what is missing, and as the remedy. AT-001.10's
 146:  * words are "the attempt is blocked with verification named as the remedy", and a 403 saying
 147:  * "forbidden" would satisfy a weaker reading of that criterion while telling the sender nothing
 148:  * about what to do next. The test body matches the reason against `/verif/i` and `/email/i`.
 149:  *
 150:  * IT IS TYPE-BLIND — see the module header. No account type reaches this function, because
 151:  * decision-8's floor is under EVERY Discovery message and AT-001.10's NGO is one instance of it.
 152:  *
 153:  * IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT VETTING. Decision-8 also says vetting is "never the Discovery wall", so a
 154:  * vetting state is not a second condition hiding in here — verification is the whole floor, and
 155:  * adding anything beside it would be building a wall the ratified text refuses.
 156:  */
 157: export function discoveryMessageAllowed(caller: DiscoveryMessageCaller): Decision<'verified'> {
 158:   if (caller?.emailVerified === true) {
 159:     return { ok: true, value: 'verified' };
 160:   }
 161:   return {
 162:     ok: false,
 163:     reason:
 164:       'a Discovery message needs a verified email address — this account is email-unverified. ' +
 165:       'Use the verification link sent to the account address, then send the message again',
 166:   };
 167: }
===== tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts =====
   1: /**
   2:  * THE ORACLE FOR `verification.ts`'s FAIL-CLOSED PROMISE.
   3:  *
   4:  * `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` promises that every unrecognised shape reads as
   5:  * UNVERIFIED and every malformed caller is REFUSED. The two acceptance bodies that drive that
   6:  * module through the fixture exercise exactly two shapes — a `null` and a valid timestamp string
   7:  * — because those are the two states a real Auth user passes through. So the promise about
   8:  * missing fields, non-strings and malformed callers would have had NO oracle at all, and a
   9:  * promise with no oracle is an untrue stated fact waiting to happen. This file is that oracle.
  10:  *
  11:  * WHY A SHIPPED MODULE'S SHAPE TEST RIDES THE SELFTEST LANE. The promise cannot arise through the
  12:  * fixture: the fixture builds well-formed user shapes, so no acceptance body can hand this module
  13:  * a number or an array without first making the fixture lie about what Auth sends. Driving the
  14:  * module DIRECTLY is the only way to reach those inputs, and a direct call from an acceptance
  15:  * body would be the thing the suite forbids — a test of a helper rather than of an application
  16:  * boundary. The harness selftest lane is where direct unit-shaped checks already live, and CI
  17:  * already runs it.
  18:  *
  19:  * IT NEEDED NO SCRIPT OR CI CHANGE, and that was verified before the file was written rather than
  20:  * hoped for afterwards: `tests/at/vitest.config.ts` line 16 includes `harness/**\/*.selftest.ts`,
  21:  * and the `at:selftest` script filters to `harness/`. `at:verify` filters to one suite directory,
  22:  * so this file never joins an acceptance run and cannot affect any id's colour.
  23:  *
  24:  * WHAT A GREEN HERE CLAIMS: that the shipped module answers as its header says for every shape
  25:  * named. WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: that GoTrue sends any of these shapes. The one real
  26:  * `/auth/v1/user` response this repository has judged is in the live proof,
  27:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` check (d).
  28:  */
  29: 
  30: import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
  31: 
  32: import {
  33:   discoveryMessageAllowed,
  34:   emailVerifiedFromUser,
  35: } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';
  36: 
  37: describe('the shipped verification module fails closed', () => {
  38:   it('reads a non-empty email_confirmed_at string as verified, and nothing else', () => {
  39:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: '2026-08-09T12:00:00.000Z' })).toBe(true);
  40:     // A different non-empty string is still verified: this module judges PRESENCE of an instant,
  41:     // and parsing the instant is nobody's job here — GoTrue wrote it.
  42:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: 'x' })).toBe(true);
  43:   });
  44: 
  45:   it('reads every malformed email_confirmed_at as UNVERIFIED', () => {
  46:     // The field is absent altogether — the shape an unconfirmed user has on some GoTrue versions,
  47:     // and also the shape of a response body a caller read from the wrong endpoint.
  48:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({}), 'a missing field must not read as verified').toBe(false);
  49:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email: 'a@example.test' }), 'a user with other fields only').toBe(false);
  50:     // `null` — the shape an unconfirmed user has, and the one the acceptance bodies exercise.
  51:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: null })).toBe(false);
  52:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: undefined })).toBe(false);
  53:     // An empty string, and a blank one. Blank is the value most likely to arrive from a broken
  54:     // serialiser, and three spaces are not an instant.
  55:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: '' })).toBe(false);
  56:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: '   ' })).toBe(false);
  57:     // Non-strings. A number is what a timestamp becomes if somebody "helpfully" converts it, and
  58:     // `true` is what it becomes if somebody stores the answer instead of the instant.
  59:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: 0 })).toBe(false);
  60:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: 1754740800000 })).toBe(false);
  61:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: true })).toBe(false);
  62:     // An object value and an array value — the shapes a nested or repeated field produces.
  63:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: {} })).toBe(false);
  64:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: { value: '2026-08-09T12:00:00.000Z' } })).toBe(false);
  65:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser({ email_confirmed_at: ['2026-08-09T12:00:00.000Z'] })).toBe(false);
  66:   });
  67: 
  68:   it('reads a user that is not an object as UNVERIFIED', () => {
  69:     // `null` is the one that matters most: `typeof null === 'object'`, so a check that forgot it
  70:     // would throw here instead of answering, and a throw on the write path is not a refusal.
  71:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser(null)).toBe(false);
  72:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser(undefined)).toBe(false);
  73:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser('2026-08-09T12:00:00.000Z')).toBe(false);
  74:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser(42)).toBe(false);
  75:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser(true)).toBe(false);
  76:     // An array has no `email_confirmed_at`, so it reads unverified rather than throwing.
  77:     expect(emailVerifiedFromUser([])).toBe(false);
  78:   });
  79: 
  80:   it('allows a Discovery message only on emailVerified === true', () => {
  81:     const allowed = discoveryMessageAllowed({ emailVerified: true });
  82:     expect(allowed.ok).toBe(true);
  83:     if (allowed.ok) expect(allowed.value).toBe('verified');
  84:   });
  85: 
  86:   it('refuses a Discovery message from an unverified caller, naming verification as the remedy', () => {
  87:     const refused = discoveryMessageAllowed({ emailVerified: false });
  88:     expect(refused.ok).toBe(false);
  89:     if (refused.ok) return;
  90:     // The same two patterns AT-001.10's body matches. Asserted here as well, so a reason that
  91:     // stopped naming the remedy fails at the module rather than only inside one acceptance body.
  92:     expect(refused.reason).toMatch(/verif/i);
  93:     expect(refused.reason).toMatch(/email/i);
  94:   });
  95: 
  96:   it('refuses a missing or malformed caller', () => {
  97:     // Every call below is a shape the type-checker would reject at a checked call site. The edge
  98:     // entry point is NOT type-checked — measured, and stated in `accounts.ts`'s header — so these
  99:     // are the mistakes a real caller can make, and the casts are how the test reaches them.
 100:     const call = (caller: unknown) =>
 101:       discoveryMessageAllowed(caller as Parameters<typeof discoveryMessageAllowed>[0]);
 102: 
 103:     expect(call(undefined).ok, 'a missing caller must be refused, never allowed').toBe(false);
 104:     expect(call(null).ok).toBe(false);
 105:     expect(call({}).ok, 'a caller with no emailVerified field must be refused').toBe(false);
 106:     // The truthy non-`true` values. A truthiness test would have allowed all four.
 107:     expect(call({ emailVerified: 'true' }).ok).toBe(false);
 108:     expect(call({ emailVerified: 1 }).ok).toBe(false);
 109:     expect(call({ emailVerified: 'yes' }).ok).toBe(false);
 110:     expect(call({ emailVerified: {} }).ok).toBe(false);
 111:     // A near-miss field name, which is what a rename leaves behind.
 112:     expect(call({ email_verified: true }).ok).toBe(false);
 113:     expect(call('verified').ok).toBe(false);
 114:   });
 115: });
