 403: /* =============================================================================================
 404:  * (g) A PLATFORM ADMINISTRATOR IS PROVISIONED AND SIGNS IN.
 405:  *
 406:  * A DEVIATION FROM THE PLAN'S WORDING, and it is the plan that has to move rather than this
 407:  * script. Plan step 7(g) says "provisioned the only legal way — a service-role write". It is NOT a
 408:  * service-role write, because the service role deliberately holds NO INSERT privilege anywhere in
 409:  * this schema: every write goes through a SECURITY DEFINER function, which is what makes
 410:  * `complete_signup`'s platform_admin refusal sit on the only write path a running service can
 411:  * reach. Granting the service role a direct insert would have made the plan's sentence true and
 412:  * the guard weaker.
 413:  *
 414:  * So provisioning is a direct database operation by an operator — a NARROWER authority than the
 415:  * service role, not a wider one. The criterion says "provisioned", and this is what provisioning
 416:  * is here. The auth user itself is still created through Auth's own admin API with the service
 417:  * key, because only Auth can mint a credential that can then sign in.
 418:  * ============================================================================================= */
